+## 69A. The Davenport–Heilbronn twin on the rung ladder and the Löwner route
+
+**The function.** Let $\kappa=(\sqrt{10-2\sqrt5}-2)/(\sqrt5-1)=0.2840790438\ldots$.
+Let $a_n$ be periodic modulo 5 with $(a_1,\ldots,a_5)=(1,\kappa,-\kappa,-1,0)$,
+and put $f(s)=\sum_{n\ge1}a_nn^{-s}=5^{-s}\sum_{r=1}^4a_r\,\zeta(s,r/5)$.
+Equivalently $f=\tfrac12(1-i\kappa)L(s,\chi)+\tfrac12(1+i\kappa)L(s,\bar\chi)$,
+where $\chi$ is the odd character modulo 5 with $\chi(2)=i$ [122; 3, §10.25].
+The completed function
+
+$$
+F(s)=(5/\pi)^{s/2}\,\Gamma\!\left(\tfrac{s+1}2\right)f(s)
+$$
+
+is entire of order one and satisfies $F(s)=F(1-s)$; since the $a_n$ are
+real, $F$ is real on the critical line. The twin shares the reflection
+$s\mapsto1-s$ of $\xi(s)=\tfrac12s(s-1)\pi^{-s/2}\Gamma(s/2)\zeta(s)$, but not its
+Gamma factor: $\Gamma((s+1)/2)$ and the conductor $5$ are those of an odd
+character. Numerically the identity holds to $2\times10^{-13}$
+in double precision and to $10^{-39}$ at 40 digits. The fold, the reflection
+orbits and the resolvent
+
+$$
+S_f(x)=\sum_{[\rho]}\frac{m_\rho}{x+q_\rho}=\frac{F'}{F}(s_x)\Big/R
+$$
+
+are defined exactly as for $\xi$. The $1/x$ term of (16.1) is absent because
+$f$ has no pole.
+
+**What it lacks.** The coefficients $c(n)$ of $-f'/f$ play the role of
+$\Lambda(n)$ in (16.2). They are signed, and they are supported off the prime
+powers:
