+## 69B. Parity, the local Gaussian form, and the detection law
+
+**Lemma 69B.1 (parity).** Let $F$ be analytic near a real point $q_r$ and real
+on the real axis, such as
+$q\mapsto q/((x_i+q)(x_j+q))$ or its powers. Then for small real $\eta$
+
+$$
+F(q_r+i\eta)+F(q_r-i\eta)
+=2\sum_{m\ge0}\frac{(-1)^m\eta^{2m}}{(2m)!}F^{(2m)}(q_r)
+=2F(q_r)-\eta^2F''(q_r)+O(\eta^4).
+\tag{TW.1}
+$$
+
+\PROVED{} *Proof.* Taylor expansion; the odd powers of $i\eta$ cancel in the
+conjugate sum. $\square$
+
+Every rung, Li coefficient, Hankel entry and Löwner entry is a sum of such
+conjugate pairs over folded nodes. An off-line pair therefore differs from
+its on-line model only at order $\eta^2$, with $\eta=\Im q_\rho$. This is the
+quantitative form of the v0.3 parity firewall (§69C).
+
+**Proposition 69B.2 (local Gaussian form).** Write $q=\tfrac14+z^2$, so that an
+off-line zero $\tfrac12+\delta+i\gamma$ has $z=\gamma-i\delta$ exactly. Put
+$x=\tfrac14+\tau^2$ and $\varepsilon=(z^2-\tau^2)/x$. Then
+
+$$
+\log\frac{4xq}{(x+q)^2}=\log(1+\varepsilon)-2\log\!\left(1+\tfrac\varepsilon2\right)
+=-\frac{\varepsilon^2}4+\frac{\varepsilon^3}4-\frac{7\varepsilon^4}{32}+O(\varepsilon^5),
+$$
+
+so with $K=k/\tau^2$,
+
+$$
+\frac{W_k(x)}{(2k-1)!\,(4x)^{-k}}
+=\sum_{[\rho]}m_\rho\,\Re\,e^{-K(z_\rho-\tau)^2\,(1+O(|z_\rho-\tau|/\tau))}.
+\tag{TW.2}
+$$
+
+\PROVED{} A high rung near height $\tau$ is thus a Gaussian smoothing of the
+zero ordinates, of width $\tau/\sqrt{2k}$. An off-line pair contributes
+$2e^{-K((\gamma-\tau)^2-\delta^2)}\cos(2K\delta(\gamma-\tau))$ at leading order.
+Its first negative band, at distance $\approx\pi/(4K\delta)$, dominates once it
+fits inside the gap to the nearest on-line zero. That gives
+$K^*\approx\pi/(2a\delta)$. Using only the zeros within $\pm12$ of zero 0,
+the exact local form reproduces $k^*=16{,}589$.
+
+**The detection law.** An off-line zero at height $t$, displacement $\delta$,
+with nearest on-line zero at distance $a$, is first seen near
+
+$$
+k^*\approx1.3\,\frac{t^2}{a\,\delta}.
+\tag{TW.3}
+$$
+
+\EVID{} The table of §69A gives a median ratio to $(\pi/2)t^2/(a\delta)$ of
+0.81 over 15 twin zeros (range 0.68–1.04). As a second calibration, two
+consecutive zeta zeros from Platt's tables were merged into one off-line pair
+at their mean height with $\delta\in\{0.02,0.1,0.3\}$, which preserves
+$N(T)$. Across 90 such plants at six heights from $1.9\times10^3$ to $10^9$,
+the median ratio is 0.82 (10–90% range 0.57–1.09). The earlier reading
+$k^*\theta^2\approx$ const is superseded: it spreads over a factor of 71 on the
+same plants.
+
+*Consequence for §R13.* $K_H=4{,}712{,}664{,}392{,}502$ is a guarantee from
+phase alone, linear in the verified height. The detection scale (TW.3) is
+quadratic. An off-line zeta zero just above the verified height, with
+$\delta\approx0.1$ and $a\approx0.1$, would first break the ladder near
+$k\sim10^{27}$.
+
+## 69C. Two firewalls restored from the v0.3 edition
+
+Both statements were proved in the v0.3 edition [124] and never retired. They
+did not appear in editions 0.4–5.1.
+
+**Theorem 69C.1 (orientation annihilation).** For $c>0$ put
+$g_c(q)=\log(|q+ic|/|q-ic|)$. Then $g_c(\bar q)=-g_c(q)$, hence
+
+$$
+\sum_{q\notin\mathbb R}m_q\,g_c(q)=0,
+\qquad
+L_\xi(c)=\tfrac12\sum_{q\notin\mathbb R}m_q|g_c(q)|,
+$$
+
+where $L_\xi(c)=\sum_{\Im q>0}m_q\,g_c(q)\ge0$ is the upper Blaschke defect.
+$L_\xi(c)=0$ exactly when RH holds. A conjugation-symmetric linear zero
+functional applied to $g_c$ therefore erases the orientation bit: absolute
+value, a half-plane projection, or equivalent phase information must be
+added. \PROVED{} [124]
+
+The defect itself is first order. For $q=Q+i\eta$ with small $\eta>0$,
+$g_c(q)=2c\eta/(Q^2+c^2)+O(\eta^2)$. Contrast (TW.1).
+
+**Theorem 69C.2 (compact Euler-ray instability).** Let
+$I=[a,b]\subset(-\infty,0)$, fix $k\ge0$ and a nonreal $q_*$ with
+$\Im q_*>0$. There are real polynomials $H_n$ with $H_n>0$ on $\mathbb R$,
+$H_n(q_*)=H_n(\bar q_*)=0$, and $H_n\to1$ in $C^k(I)$. Consequently, for any
+real-symmetric Cartwright entire function $X$ of order $\tfrac12$,
+$Y_n=XH_n$ stays in the same class, $Y_n/X\to1$ in $C^k(I)$, and
+$L_{Y_n}(c)-L_X(c)\ge\log|(q_*+ic)/(q_*-ic)|>0$. \PROVED{} [124]
+
+*Proof sketch.* Map $I$ affinely to $[-1,1]$ and put $\zeta=\phi(q_*)$. Real
+combinations $p_n=A_nT_n+B_nT_{n+1}$ with $p_n(q_*)=i$ have
+$|A_n|+|B_n|=O(|\lambda|^{-n})$, where $\lambda=\zeta+\sqrt{\zeta^2-1}$ and
+$|\lambda|>1$. So $p_n\to0$ in $C^k(I)$ by Markov's inequality. Set
+$H_n=1+p_n^2$. $\square$
+
+The perturbations do not preserve an Euler product. The theorem rules out
+continuous compact-ray extraction from general analytic structure, not a
+zeta-specific arithmetic identity. The twin of §69A realizes the same lesson
+with an actual Dirichlet series that satisfies the functional equation.
 
